body:
`sail get`'s streaming arm (`get.rs`, `Backend::Stream`) frames every record up to the largest position asked for with `Reader::advance()`. It keeps the records inside a requested range and counts as it goes:

```rust
let mut n = 0;
while n < last && reader.advance()? {
    if wanted.iter().any(|&(f, l)| n + 1 >= f && n < l) {
        held[n] = Some(reader.record().to_vec());
    }
    n += 1;
}
if n < last {
    bail!("position {last} was asked for and the input holds {n} records")
}
```

sail-317db4 added `Reader::advance_to(n)`. With it, the loop could walk only the requested positions:

```rust
// ascending and without repeats, the order advance_to needs
let positions: BTreeSet<usize> = wanted
    .iter()
    .flat_map(|&(first, last)| first - 1..last)
    .collect();

for &at in &positions {
    if !reader.advance_to(at)? {
        bail!("position {last} was asked for and the input holds {} records", ???)
    }
    held[at] = Some(reader.record().to_vec());
}
```

Both loops read the same bytes, since `advance_to` frames every record it passes. The one obstacle is the `???`. When `advance_to` returns `false`, it has framed the rest of the input without handing any of it to `get`, and `position()` is `None`. Take `get 3,500` on a 40-record file. `get` learns only that the input holds at least 3 records. A counter in `get` cannot recover the number, and stdin cannot be read twice. The Indexed and Memory arms print the same message from `collection.len()`, so the count is part of what every backend writes.

## Options

| option | `libsail` | `sail` command line | the error tells the user |
|---|---|---|---|
| leave `get` as it is | none | none | the count |
| a public count of records framed, the private `framed` field exposed | new method | none | the count |
| wait for sail-427b32 to pick keep last, so `position() + 1` is the count | none beyond that decision | none | the count |
| drop the count from the message in all three backends | none | message changes | only that the input is short |

Leaning: leave `get` as it is until sail-427b32 is decided. If it picks keep last, the third option costs nothing more. Whether the `advance_to` loop reads more clearly than today's is the open judgment.
